# Change request protocol

Accepted requirements and tech design are settled. During implementation they change only through a
change request (CR) the developer decides, and every CR stays visible: in the brief, your final
message, the Dev QA report and the pull request.

1. Stop work on the affected IDs; mark their rows in `## Implementation` as `blocked by CR-n`.
2. Write the entry in the brief's `## Change requests` (template below).
3. Decide whether the change can be made in place or must go back (table below); note it in **Impact**.
4. For an in-place option, draft the exact new text and make it pass the re-check before you ask.
5. Ask the developer and record the decision.
6. Act on it: commit the edit, keep the item, go back, or stay pending.

## When to raise one

| Situation | What to do |
|---|---|
| A rule or scenario is wrong, contradicts another one, or is impossible or too costly to satisfy | CR |
| A tech design requirement cannot be met, or its cost is out of proportion | CR |
| The work needs a business rule or technical requirement the accepted files lack, even one the request asks for | CR |
| A test that matches an accepted scenario would have to change | CR on the scenario |
| A rule reads two ways | ask which reading holds; a CR only when the answer changes the wording |
| Dev QA reported leakage (a ticket key, requester name or request sentence) in a requirement or tech design file | CR, wording only: IDs and meaning kept, the re-check below passes; in place |
| How to build something the tech design leaves open | no CR: your decision |
| A violation in a feature outside the task | no CR: a follow-up under **Follow-ups and known gaps** in `## Hand-off to the QA team` |

## Entry template

Number CRs in order; never reuse a number.

```markdown
### CR-n: <one-line summary>

- **Artifact:** <path> · **ID:** <ID> · **Raised:** implement-task, <date>
- **Current:** "<exact current text>"
- **Proposed:** "<exact new text>" | retire <ID>
- **Evidence:** <test output, code path, constraint, measurement or cost that shows the problem>
- **Options:**
  1. Keep as written. Pros: … Cons: …
  2. <edit in place>. Pros: … Cons: …
  3. <revise in Behavior or Tech design>. Pros: … Cons: …
- **Recommendation:** <option>, because …
- **Impact:** <IDs, tests, other features, tech design sections>; in place | go back (<trigger>)
- **Decision:** pending | accepted in place <date> | kept as written <date> | sent back with <event> <date>, resolved by <commit>
```

Filled example:

```markdown
### CR-2: A refund cannot promise the money within 1 day

- **Artifact:** {{vars.requirementsDir}}/refunds/{{vars.requirementsFile}} · **ID:** REF-3.1 · **Raised:** implement-task, 2026-10-07
- **Current:** "Then the Customer receives the Refund on the original Payment Method within 1 day"
- **Proposed:** "Then the Refund is issued to the original Payment Method within 1 day"
- **Evidence:** the Payment Provider settles refunds in 3 to 5 business days (constraint REF-CON-1);
  the shop controls only when the Refund is issued, so no implementation can pass the scenario.
- **Options:**
  1. Keep as written. Pros: no requirement change. Cons: untestable promise; Dev QA fails it every round.
  2. Reword REF-3.1 as proposed. Pros: true and testable. Cons: the Customer is not told when the
     money arrives.
  3. Revise in Behavior with a new Rule that tells the Customer the settlement time. Pros: sets the
     right expectation. Cons: a new Rule, another acceptance round.
- **Recommendation:** 2, with option 3 as a follow-up.
- **Impact:** REF-3.1 and its test; no other feature, no tech design section; in place (one scenario).
- **Decision:** accepted in place 2026-10-07
```

## In place or go back

Edit in place by default. Go back instead when any trigger holds, or whenever the developer prefers:

| Trigger | Event |
|---|---|
| A new Rule or a new feature folder is needed | `REVISE_REQUIREMENTS` |
| A term of the shared GLOSSARY.md, or another feature's requirements, would change | `REVISE_REQUIREMENTS` |
| More than about 3 scenarios are affected | `REVISE_REQUIREMENTS` |
| The class or route changes: the task is another class after all, or the edit touches a file whose step the route skipped (requirements on a `code` or `design` route, tech design on a `code` or `behavior` route) | `REVISE_REQUIREMENTS` for requirements, `REVISE_DESIGN` for tech design |
| A decision `DEC-n` falls, or another feature's tech design or the project baseline would change | `REVISE_DESIGN` |
| The change needs persisted data, an interface, a UI placement or a quality target no tech design ID covers, or contradicts an N/A verdict of the screening | `REVISE_DESIGN` |

## Re-check the proposed text

Before you ask, the proposed text passes the checks of the step that owns the file; after the edit,
confirm the file still passes them.

**Requirements file (the Behavior gate checklist):**

- [ ] A reworded rule or scenario keeps its ID; a replaced one gets a new ID and the old one is
      retired, never reused.
- [ ] IDs are well formed (`REF-3`, `REF-3.1`, the first word of the name) and unique.
- [ ] Every Rule still has at least one Scenario, plus a negative one when it rejects something.
- [ ] Each scenario: one behavior, one When/Then pair, 3 to 5 steps, glossary roles as actors in the
      third person, errors as business outcomes, no UI or technical words (page, button, click, API,
      database, …) unless the glossary defines them as domain terms.
- [ ] Every domain term resolves to the local glossary; a shared term's copy matches GLOSSARY.md.
- [ ] No rule of the feature contradicts the edited one.
- [ ] No ticket keys, requester names or request sentences; change-relative words (new, now, no
      longer, currently, will) shown to the developer in the question.
- [ ] The index row and the `Coverage:` line stay true.
- [ ] No-technical-impact check: the edit changes no persisted data, interface, UI placement or
      quality target. A hit needs a tech design change too: a second CR, or go back per the table.

**Tech design file (the tech design format):**

- [ ] One-liner `[While <state>,] [When <trigger>,] <subject> shall <response> <measure>.` in a row
      `ID | Sub-characteristic | Requirement | Measure | Verify by | Priority | Source`.
- [ ] Verify by is test, bench, static, inspect or ops; Priority is Must or Should; Source is user,
      existing, baseline, proposed or assumed (kept by a fast-forward run).
- [ ] A reworded requirement keeps its ID and catalog code (`REF-PERF-1`); a replaced one gets a new
      ID and the old one moves to the Retired IDs section with its reason (the commit names the CR).
- [ ] Logical level: no file, class or library version names; a technology only as a constraint. UI
      rows state placement, not looks, unless the task has the `ui-design` modifier.
- [ ] The row agrees with the baseline `SYS-` items it refines and with the brief's screening.
- [ ] No ticket keys, requester names, request sentences or task progress.
- [ ] The grill below passes; a number you proposed becomes `Source: user` once the developer
      confirms it in the decision (a fast-forward run keeps it as `Source: assumed`).

## Grill a tech design item

Grill the proposed requirement and every requirement of its section it could conflict with. Put the
grill questions into the same message as the CR options; the CR decision accepts the section.

{{> td-grill}}

## Ask the developer

Send the entry as a chat message: the problem in one sentence, the options with pros and cons, your
recommendation, and "Which option? (default: <recommendation>)". Only an explicit answer decides:
"2", "go with your recommendation", "keep it". Silence, a change of topic or an "ok" to something else
is not a decision. Record the answer and the date in **Decision**; a fast-forward run takes the Recommendation and writes ` (auto)` after the date.

## Act on the decision

- **Accepted in place:** edit the file, confirm the re-check, and commit the file and the brief (the
  CR entry with its decision) together and nothing else: `docs(req): CR-n <summary>` or
  `docs(td): CR-n <summary>`. Dev QA matches every requirement or tech design commit after
  acceptance to an accepted CR by that `CR-n`, so never fold a CR edit into another commit. Adapt
  tests and code in the following behavior commits naming the edited ID; a retired ID's test is
  deleted there, citing the CR.
- **Kept as written:** implement the item as written. If that stays impossible, say so; never fake a pass.
- **Sent back:** append a Rework entry (the event, implement-task, numbered items citing `CR-n`
  with the proposed change and the trigger), commit the brief
  (`docs(task): CR-n sent back for <task>`) and fire the event. When the work returns, fill in
  **resolved by** with the owning step's acceptance commit and resume the plan.
- **No developer reachable** (never in a fast-forward run): leave the decision pending, commit the brief
  (`docs(task): CR-n pending for <task>`), say what blocks the task and stop without firing an
  event. The next session starts with this CR.

Your final message lists every CR: number, artifact and ID, one-line change, decision.
